 {
  "key": "discrete.random-variables.q17",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.discrete.random-variables"
  ],
  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 4,
  "estimatedSeconds": 360,
  "stem": "$X$ has a discrete uniform distribution on $1, 2, \\ldots, n$. Using $\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r = \\frac{1}{2}n(n + 1)$ and $\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^2 = \\frac{1}{6}n(n + 1)(2n + 1)$, show that $\\mathrm{E}(X) = \\frac{n + 1}{2}$ and $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = \\frac{n^2 - 1}{12}$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 $\\mathrm{E}(X) = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum r = \\frac{n + 1}{2}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 $\\mathrm{E}(X^2) = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum r^2 = \\frac{(n + 1)(2n + 1)}{6}$.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "M1 $\\mathrm{Var}(X) = \\frac{(n + 1)(2n + 1)}{6} - \\frac{(n + 1)^2}{4}$ with a common denominator 12.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p4",
      "description": "A1 simplifies $\\frac{(n + 1)(4n + 2 - 3n - 3)}{12} = \\frac{(n + 1)(n - 1)}{12}$ to the given result.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\n\\mathrm{E}(X) = \\sum_{r=1}^{n} r \\cdot \\frac{1}{n} = \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} = \\frac{n + 1}{2}\n$$\n$$\n\\mathrm{E}(X^2) = \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot \\frac{n(n + 1)(2n + 1)}{6} = \\frac{(n + 1)(2n + 1)}{6}\n$$\n$$\n\\mathrm{Var}(X) = \\frac{(n + 1)(2n + 1)}{6} - \\frac{(n + 1)^2}{4} = \\frac{(n + 1)\\left[2(2n + 1) - 3(n + 1)\\right]}{12}\n$$\n$$\n= \\frac{(n + 1)(n - 1)}{12} = \\frac{n^2 - 1}{12}\n$$"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "Each value has probability 1/n, so the sums of r and r² give the moments.",
  "hints": [
   "Each probability is 1/n.",
   "Take out the factor (n + 1) before simplifying."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "applies": [
   "zimsec/a-level/statistics"
  ],
  "calculator": false
 },
